WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.960
In machine learning, one of the best ways to learn&nbsp;
more about your data is by classifying it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.480
what you already know. You can group together a&nbsp;
bunch of data based on similar characteristics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.920
Because you already know these characteristics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.200
you can classify the data using&nbsp;
supervised machine learning.&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.080
A very common supervised machine&nbsp;
learning algorithm for multiclass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.680
classification is k-Nearest Neighbor. This is&nbsp;
an instance-based machine learning algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.520
or what's also called lazy&nbsp;
learning. With lazy learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.840
the bulk of the computation happens right&nbsp;
before you want to classify your data.&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.880
The learning doesn't happen continuously. Instead,&nbsp;
you run all your computation in one big instance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.520
In a sense, you're saving up all your energy&nbsp;
for one big splash. k-NN compares something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.120
you don't know to what you already have. So, in&nbsp;
some ways, you're getting immediately rewarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.920
for the size and quality of your training&nbsp;
data. The downside is that this takes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.680
lot of computational power, so sometimes it's&nbsp;
difficult to use k-NN on very large data sets.&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:25.200
Think of it this way. For a veterinary doctor, one&nbsp;
of the most difficult jobs was trying to classify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.680
the breed of each new dog. There are hundreds&nbsp;
of different known dog breeds. Not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.320
the dogs aren't that close-minded about who they&nbsp;
breed with, so you have a lot of mixed breeds.&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.320
Each time when there is a new dog, the veterinary&nbsp;
doctor would hold it up to several of the existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.120
dogs that were already classified. Then&nbsp;
we'd look at some of the characteristics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.800
Maybe it was the shape of their face&nbsp;
or the color of their hair. In a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.120
the purpose was trying to classify the unknown&nbsp;
dog by looking for its nearest neighbor.&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.080
Another way to look at it is you're&nbsp;
trying to minimize the distance between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.360
the unknown dog and the known breeds. If&nbsp;
the characteristics were closely matched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.440
then there was a very short distance between&nbsp;
the unknown dog and its nearest neighbor.&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.520
Minimizing the distance is a key part of&nbsp;
k-Nearest Neighbor. The closer you are to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.640
your nearest neighbors, the more likely you&nbsp;
are to be accurate. The most common way to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.440
this is through something called Euclidean&nbsp;
distance. There is a pretty sophisticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.840
mathematical formula that can help see the&nbsp;
distance between your different data points.&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.760
Now, imagine you had millions of dogs and you&nbsp;
wanted to classify them based on their breed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.320
To start out, we might want to create two key&nbsp;
characteristics. These will help you classify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:56.960
dogs that share the same breed. These are often&nbsp;
called predictors. So, let's use their weight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.880
the length of their hair. Now let's take these&nbsp;
two characteristics and put them on an X-Y axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.560
diagram. Let's put the length of their hair along&nbsp;
the Y-axis and their weight along the X-axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.360
Let's take 1,000 classified dogs in our training&nbsp;
set. We'll put them on the graph based on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.560
weight and hair length. Now let's take our&nbsp;
unknown dog and put it in the same chart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.520
You can see that it's not matched with another&nbsp;
dog, but it has a bunch of close neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:32.240
Let's say we use a K of five. That means we'd&nbsp;
want to put a circle around our unclassified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.080
dog and its five closest neighbors. You can see&nbsp;
that if the distance of the other dogs is shorter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.040
you'll probably get a much&nbsp;
more accurate classification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.960
Now let's look at the five closest neighbors.&nbsp;
You'll see that three of them are Huskies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.680
two of them are Shepherds You can be somewhat&nbsp;
confident to classify your unknown dog as a Husky.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.640
k-Nearest Neighbor is a very common and&nbsp;
powerful machine learning algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.600
That's because it can do more&nbsp;
than just sort dogs. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:13.840
it's commonly used in finance to look for the&nbsp;
best stocks and even predict future performance.

